WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.807
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path:

2
00:00:03.807 --> 00:00:05.450
one writer per attribute.

3
00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.809
This module follows the guide's Gate 2,

4
00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:12.070
which settles two decisions on
paper before anything is connected:

5
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:16.788
who writes each attribute, and which
system approves each authority class.

6
00:00:16.788 --> 00:00:20.190
Then you'll use the developer
workbook's Chapter 08 check,

7
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.824
which reads two provisioning
configurations, Entra inbound provisioning's

8
00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:28.588
and SailPoint's, and reports every
attribute both of them write.

9
00:00:28.588 --> 00:00:32.435
You'll run it on fictional exports,
add an alias, and see why the

10
00:00:32.435 --> 00:00:37.260
alias list and the directory name
are inputs a reviewer has to confirm.

11
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.760
Why does this module exist?

12
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:43.885
Here's the story.

13
00:00:43.885 --> 00:00:47.469
Entra inbound provisioning
writes department from HR.

14
00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:50.898
SailPoint's Entra connector
also writes department.

15
00:00:50.898 --> 00:00:52.948
Each sync overwrites the other.

16
00:00:52.948 --> 00:00:56.592
And the drift you'll measure in
Module 4 comes back the next day,

17
00:00:56.592 --> 00:00:58.735
no matter how often someone fixes it.

18
00:00:58.735 --> 00:01:01.214
The reconciliation sees the result,

19
00:01:01.214 --> 00:01:05.097
attribute drift that keeps
coming back, but not the cause.

20
00:01:05.097 --> 00:01:07.770
Fixing the value by hand never settles it,

21
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.522
because the next sync recreates the drift.

22
00:01:10.522 --> 00:01:14.105
Gate 2 settles it on paper,
before anything is connected:

23
00:01:14.105 --> 00:01:18.622
one writer per attribute, and one
approver system per authority class.

24
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:20.910
In this module you learn those decisions,

25
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:25.180
and you check two provisioning
configurations against the first one.

26
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.721
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

27
00:01:31.721 --> 00:01:34.068
which takes about forty
minutes with the lab.

28
00:01:34.068 --> 00:01:37.167
You'll explain the two writer
patterns Gate 2 allows,

29
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.618
and why it allows only
one of them at a time.

30
00:01:39.618 --> 00:01:44.256
You'll explain why Gate 2 names one
approver system for each authority class.

31
00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:48.117
You'll run the writer check
over two configuration exports,

32
00:01:48.117 --> 00:01:51.742
and interpret each Finding,
Warn and Info line it prints.

33
00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:55.284
And you'll verify that the alias
list and the directory name

34
00:01:55.284 --> 00:01:58.681
are inputs a reviewer checks,
not things the tool guesses.

35
00:01:58.681 --> 00:02:00.517
You need Module 2 first.

36
00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:06.304
For the Graph export in Lab 2, the Microsoft
Automation path's first module helps.

37
00:02:06.304 --> 00:02:09.760
Lab 1 runs on fixtures, with no tenant.

38
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.162
Used as shipped, the two products fall
short of the loop in four places,

39
00:02:18.162 --> 00:02:20.610
and Gate 2 closes two of them on paper.

40
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.481
Gap two: with SailPoint's
Service Catalog app,

41
00:02:23.481 --> 00:02:27.437
requests follow the approval
process defined in ServiceNow,

42
00:02:27.437 --> 00:02:32.790
while the Federal Modernization Workbook's
design decides owner-governed access,

43
00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:36.747
classes A two and A three, in
the identity governance platform.

44
00:02:36.747 --> 00:02:40.511
Gate 2 answers with one approver
system per authority class.

45
00:02:40.511 --> 00:02:43.347
Gap four: two doors into the directory.

46
00:02:43.347 --> 00:02:48.346
SailPoint can provision Entra ID and
Active Directory, while the workbook's

47
00:02:48.346 --> 00:02:53.822
design makes Entra inbound provisioning
the one writer of HR-mastered attributes.

48
00:02:53.822 --> 00:02:56.784
Gate 2 answers with one
writer per attribute.

49
00:02:56.784 --> 00:03:00.897
It also gives each operation class
a lane and a change-record type.

50
00:03:00.897 --> 00:03:04.110
Every later gate builds
what this one decides.

51
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:11.064
Gate 2's first step is to choose
one writer per attribute,

52
00:03:11.064 --> 00:03:14.425
and the guide offers two
patterns, as design guidance.

53
00:03:14.425 --> 00:03:17.925
In the first, Entra inbound
provisioning stays the door.

54
00:03:17.925 --> 00:03:22.733
SailPoint aggregates HR read-only,
and governs entitlements:

55
00:03:22.733 --> 00:03:26.975
groups, roles, access packages
and application access.

56
00:03:26.975 --> 00:03:30.615
In the second, SailPoint becomes
the provisioning service,

57
00:03:30.615 --> 00:03:34.850
and takes on the duties the workbook's
design gives the HR middleware.

58
00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:36.562
Pick one and write it down.

59
00:03:36.562 --> 00:03:37.537
Not both.

60
00:03:37.537 --> 00:03:41.788
Then record each attribute's writer
in the program's attribute map.

61
00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:47.505
One fact helps the choice: SailPoint's
Oracle HCM Cloud connector reads Core HCM

62
00:03:47.505 --> 00:03:50.623
directly, without an
on-premises appliance,

63
00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:54.088
and writes back only the
user name and work email.

64
00:03:54.088 --> 00:03:59.740
For the first pattern's build, the guide
points to its Oracle HCM to Entra guide.

65
00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:07.153
Step two chooses one approver
system per authority class.

66
00:04:07.153 --> 00:04:09.658
The guide's example, design guidance:

67
00:04:09.658 --> 00:04:14.502
ServiceNow approves A zero and A one
through its Ask for Approval flow.

68
00:04:14.502 --> 00:04:16.967
SailPoint approves A two and A three,

69
00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:22.227
through access-request approvals by an
owner, a manager or a governance group.

70
00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:26.741
The Service Catalog app only
collects A two and A three requests.

71
00:04:26.741 --> 00:04:31.565
And every approval's timeout outcome
is reject or cancel, never approve.

72
00:04:31.565 --> 00:04:36.602
Step three gives each operation class a
lane, following the workbook's design.

73
00:04:36.602 --> 00:04:39.815
The mapping onto these products
is the program's inference.

74
00:04:39.815 --> 00:04:42.730
An autonomous class runs
as a standard change,

75
00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.565
and the authorizing official
accepts any class run that way.

76
00:04:46.565 --> 00:04:49.853
A gated class is an access
request with approval.

77
00:04:49.853 --> 00:04:54.330
Step four writes the decisions to
the record as a reviewed change.

78
00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:03.252
The guide names the automated check for
Gate 2, built in the workbook's Chapter 08.

79
00:05:03.252 --> 00:05:04.090
Why now?

80
00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:09.079
The implementation example planned a
read-only check of the two provisioning

81
00:05:09.079 --> 00:05:13.531
mappings for Pass 2, and both
configurations export as JSON through

82
00:05:13.531 --> 00:05:17.215
documented reads, so the check
runs on files, offline, today.

83
00:05:17.215 --> 00:05:18.903
It reads two exports.

84
00:05:18.903 --> 00:05:23.665
The first is the inbound job's
synchronization schema, from Microsoft Graph.

85
00:05:23.665 --> 00:05:28.950
It takes the rules that target the Entra
directory, the enabled mappings to User,

86
00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:33.194
and each attribute's flow type:
Always updates it on every change,

87
00:05:33.194 --> 00:05:36.077
and Object Add Only sets
it only at creation.

88
00:05:36.077 --> 00:05:39.564
The second is the SailPoint
source's provisioning policies.

89
00:05:39.564 --> 00:05:42.423
An Update policy is used
for attribute sync,

90
00:05:42.423 --> 00:05:47.630
a Create policy when the account is
made, and other usage types are ignored.

91
00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:56.180
For each attribute both systems write,
the check gives one of three results.

92
00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:01.530
A Finding when both update it: each overwrites
the other, and the command exits one.

93
00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.251
A Warn when one updates it and
the other sets it at creation:

94
00:06:05.251 --> 00:06:07.267
two sources for its first value.

95
00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:10.130
And Info when both set
it only at creation.

96
00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.892
Two inputs need a reviewer.

97
00:06:11.892 --> 00:06:13.842
First, the alias list.

98
00:06:13.842 --> 00:06:18.742
SailPoint's field names are the
connector's, not necessarily Entra ID's.

99
00:06:18.742 --> 00:06:21.500
The check matches names
case-insensitively,

100
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.256
and takes an alias for
each pair that differs.

101
00:06:24.256 --> 00:06:25.443
It doesn't guess.

102
00:06:25.443 --> 00:06:28.286
Which connector field writes
which Entra attribute

103
00:06:28.286 --> 00:06:30.742
is something to confirm
in the source's schema.

104
00:06:30.742 --> 00:06:32.731
Second, the directory name.

105
00:06:32.731 --> 00:06:36.096
A wrong name would make every
attribute look unwritten,

106
00:06:36.096 --> 00:06:39.610
so the check refuses a name
the schema doesn't target.

107
00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.685
Here's what the check guards against,
from Chapter 08's dangers table.

108
00:06:47.685 --> 00:06:52.817
Two systems updating one attribute
means drift that's fixed and recreated

109
00:06:52.817 --> 00:06:56.873
on every sync, forever; that's a
Finding, and an exit code of one.

110
00:06:56.873 --> 00:06:59.927
Field names that differ
can hide a second writer,

111
00:06:59.927 --> 00:07:04.510
so the check would report no overlap;
the guard is the reviewed alias list.

112
00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:09.698
A wrong directory name makes every attribute
look unwritten; the check refuses it.

113
00:07:09.698 --> 00:07:12.716
A disabled mapping counted
would give false findings,

114
00:07:12.716 --> 00:07:14.997
so disabled object mappings are skipped.

115
00:07:14.997 --> 00:07:19.035
And here's what it misses: writers
outside provisioning policies.

116
00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:20.948
A person has to confirm those.

117
00:07:20.948 --> 00:07:23.223
The guide is clear about scope, too.

118
00:07:23.223 --> 00:07:24.860
The check reads exports.

119
00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.826
It doesn't check approver
systems or lanes;

120
00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:30.990
those stay a manual validation of Gate 2.

121
00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.465
Try it.

122
00:07:35.465 --> 00:07:39.312
Lab 1 runs in the kit's folder
against two fictional exports:

123
00:07:39.312 --> 00:07:43.315
an HR-to-Entra inbound job, and
a SailPoint source that creates

124
00:07:43.315 --> 00:07:47.240
accounts and updates department,
title and office location.

125
00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.678
Step one runs the
chapter's tests: six pass.

126
00:07:50.678 --> 00:07:52.978
Step two checks the two exports.

127
00:07:52.978 --> 00:07:57.661
Entra inbound writes eight attributes
and SailPoint writes seven,

128
00:07:57.661 --> 00:08:00.253
with one Finding, one Warn and two Info.

129
00:08:00.253 --> 00:08:02.870
The Finding is department: both update it,

130
00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:06.489
so each overwrites the other
and the drift never settles.

131
00:08:06.489 --> 00:08:07.990
The command exits one.

132
00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:13.565
Step three maps SailPoint's title to
Entra ID's job title, and runs it again.

133
00:08:13.565 --> 00:08:16.927
Now there are two Findings:
job title joins department.

134
00:08:16.927 --> 00:08:21.260
Without the alias, the second
writer of job title was invisible.

135
00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:27.510
Step four shows the directory-name guard.

136
00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:32.460
The fixture's rules target Azure Active
Directory, the name Learn's examples use.

137
00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.452
Ask for Microsoft Entra ID instead,
and the check stops with an

138
00:08:36.452 --> 00:08:40.522
error that names the targets the
schema does have, and exits two.

139
00:08:40.522 --> 00:08:43.119
That matters, because a wrong name would

140
00:08:43.119 --> 00:08:46.110
otherwise look like no
overlap at all, and pass.

141
00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.310
Step five lists the reads
the two exports need.

142
00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.281
On SailPoint, the provisioning
policy read scope,

143
00:08:52.281 --> 00:08:54.672
for the source's provisioning policies.

144
00:08:54.672 --> 00:08:57.119
On Graph, the custom security attribute

145
00:08:57.119 --> 00:09:00.234
provisioning read permission,
for the job's schema.

146
00:09:00.234 --> 00:09:04.165
Both are reads, and the writer
check itself reads files only:

147
00:09:04.165 --> 00:09:06.972
you export the two configurations by hand.

148
00:09:06.972 --> 00:09:10.254
Check your work: given two
exports and an alias list,

149
00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:13.570
you can say which attributes
have two writers.

150
00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:20.020
Lab 2 runs the check on your own exports.

151
00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.606
You need a test Entra tenant with
an inbound provisioning job,

152
00:09:23.606 --> 00:09:26.208
and a SailPoint sandbox
with an Entra source.

153
00:09:26.208 --> 00:09:27.770
Both reads are GET only.

154
00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.502
Export the inbound job's
schema from the tenant,

155
00:09:30.502 --> 00:09:34.145
and the Entra source's provisioning
policies from the sandbox.

156
00:09:34.145 --> 00:09:36.367
Build the alias list, run the check,

157
00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:40.733
and hand each Finding to the data
architect, who decides the writer.

158
00:09:40.733 --> 00:09:44.788
Export mappings only: a schema
with expressions is fine,

159
00:09:44.788 --> 00:09:47.257
but a policy with values may not be.

160
00:09:47.257 --> 00:09:52.389
Back it out in step five: delete the exports
if they hold anything beyond mappings,

161
00:09:52.389 --> 00:09:56.295
and remove the Graph permission
and the SailPoint API client.

162
00:09:56.295 --> 00:10:02.110
The authors haven't run Lab 2; follow
Chapter 08's Lab 2 step by step.

163
00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.172
Where are agencies?

164
00:10:07.172 --> 00:10:10.723
The guide assumes one of two
starting points for the HR feed.

165
00:10:10.723 --> 00:10:15.438
Either the workbook's HR pipeline,
Entra inbound provisioning from HR,

166
00:10:15.438 --> 00:10:19.422
is already live, or accounts
are still created from tickets.

167
00:10:19.422 --> 00:10:23.160
Either way, Gate 2 decides
who writes each attribute.

168
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.360
For availability, Gate 2 uses nothing new.

169
00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.904
If API-driven inbound
provisioning is the writer,

170
00:10:29.904 --> 00:10:35.696
Microsoft Learn's feature availability
page lists it in Azure for U S Government,

171
00:10:35.696 --> 00:10:38.203
as are PIM, entitlement management,

172
00:10:38.203 --> 00:10:42.785
access reviews and Lifecycle
Workflows, read on November 1, 2026.

173
00:10:42.785 --> 00:10:46.973
In GCC, Entra ID is
the commercial service.

174
00:10:46.973 --> 00:10:50.340
The interim path: where the
HR pipeline isn't live yet,

175
00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.098
SailPoint can't be measured
against a source of authority.

176
00:10:54.098 --> 00:11:00.350
Decide the writers now, and run Gates 3 to
6 against the HR extract the kit reads.

177
00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.487
The federal requirement.

178
00:11:05.487 --> 00:11:10.063
The guide maps Gate 2 to NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three.

179
00:11:10.063 --> 00:11:14.255
A C five, separation of duties:
one approver system per class,

180
00:11:14.255 --> 00:11:16.651
and the requester never approves.

181
00:11:16.651 --> 00:11:19.428
C M three, configuration change control:

182
00:11:19.428 --> 00:11:23.300
each operation class has a
lane and a change-record type.

183
00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.007
A C two, account management:

184
00:11:25.007 --> 00:11:29.662
one writer per attribute keeps account
changes traceable to the source.

185
00:11:29.662 --> 00:11:32.732
And the workbook's design
requires the authorizing

186
00:11:32.732 --> 00:11:35.801
official to accept any
autonomous class in writing.

187
00:11:35.801 --> 00:11:40.451
Chapter 08 maps the check to
configuration settings, change control,

188
00:11:40.451 --> 00:11:44.775
impact analysis, continuous
monitoring and system monitoring.

189
00:11:44.775 --> 00:11:50.030
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the attribute
map with each writer, the approver

190
00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:55.570
and lane tables, and the check's output
with the data architect's decisions.

191
00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:02.580
Check yourself with two questions
from the module's knowledge check.

192
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.980
First: why did adding the alias from
title to job title add a Finding?

193
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:07.926
The answer is a.

194
00:12:07.926 --> 00:12:10.762
SailPoint's field names
are the connector's,

195
00:12:10.762 --> 00:12:15.017
so without the alias the second
writer of job title was invisible.

196
00:12:15.017 --> 00:12:19.843
The check doesn't guess name pairs;
the alias list is a reviewed input.

197
00:12:19.843 --> 00:12:23.179
Second: Gate 2 offers two writer patterns.

198
00:12:23.179 --> 00:12:24.843
Which choice does it rule out?

199
00:12:24.843 --> 00:12:28.114
The answer is c: both
Entra inbound provisioning

200
00:12:28.114 --> 00:12:31.305
and SailPoint writing
HR-mastered attributes.

201
00:12:31.305 --> 00:12:33.468
In the guide's words, not both.

202
00:12:33.468 --> 00:12:38.740
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

203
00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:43.315
To sum up.

204
00:12:43.315 --> 00:12:48.089
You learned Gate 2's decisions: one
writer per attribute, one approver system

205
00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:53.015
per authority class, and a lane and
change-record type for each operation class.

206
00:12:53.015 --> 00:12:58.090
You checked two provisioning configurations
for attributes with two writers,

207
00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:00.627
and read each Finding, Warn and Info line.

208
00:13:00.627 --> 00:13:03.553
And you saw why the alias
list and the directory

209
00:13:03.553 --> 00:13:06.103
name are inputs a reviewer has to confirm.

210
00:13:06.103 --> 00:13:09.317
Before you ship, work through
the Chapter 08 checklist,

211
00:13:09.317 --> 00:13:13.603
and run the check whenever either
provisioning configuration changes.

212
00:13:13.603 --> 00:13:18.170
Next is Module 4, sources and the
starting drift: Gate 3 connects HR,

213
00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:23.415
Entra ID and SailPoint on one key, and
you'll measure how far the directory is

214
00:13:23.415 --> 00:13:28.880
from HR over two days, and compare the
runs without hiding what wasn't measured.
